    <item>
      <title>SQLite JSON at Full Index Speed Using Generated Columns</title>
      <link>https://www.dbpro.app/blog/sqlite-json-virtual-columns-indexing</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Store Raw JSON First, create a simple table with a JSON column: ⚡Loading SQL environment. Let's add virtual generated columns. Suddenly your JSON behaves like normal relational columns with full index support.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46243904"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;HN Discussion&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href='https://www.dbpro.app/blog/sqlite-json-virtual-columns-indexing'&gt;Read Original Article&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Top 13 Comments:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;moregrist:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;Generated columns are pretty great, but what I would really love is a Postgres-style gin index, which dramatically speeds up json queries for unanticipated  keys.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;N_Lens:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;What a neat trick, I love SQLite as well.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;meindnoch:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;In the 2nd section you&amp;#x27;re using a CREATE TABLE plus three separate ALTER TABLE calls to add the virtual columns. In the 3rd section you&amp;#x27;re using a single CREATE TABLE with the virtual columns included from the get go.&lt;p&gt;Why?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ellisv:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I wish devs would normalize their data rather than shove everything into a JSON(B) column, especially when there is a consistent schema across records.&lt;p&gt;It&amp;#x27;s much harder to setup proper indexes, enforce constraints, and adds overhead every time you actually want to use the data.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AlexErrant:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I was looking for a way to index a JSON column that contains a JSON array, like a list of tags. AFAIK this method won&amp;#x27;t work for that; you&amp;#x27;ll either need to use FTS or a separate &amp;quot;tag&amp;quot; table that you index.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;baq:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;My understanding is Snowflake works kinda like that behind the scenes right?&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;bushbaba:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;For smaller datasets (100s of thousands of rows) I don’t see why you wouldn’t just use json columns with generated column&amp;#x2F;index where needed&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;jelder:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I thought this was common practice, generated columns for JSON performance. I&amp;#x27;ve even used this (although it was in Postgres) to maintain foreign key constraints where the key is buried in a JSON column. What we were doing was slightly cursed but it worked perfectly.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;simonw:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;Tiny bug report: I couldn&amp;#x27;t edit text in those SQL editor widgets from my iPhone, and I couldn&amp;#x27;t scroll them to see text that extended past the width of the page either.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;mring33621:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;IIRC, Vertica had&amp;#x2F;has a similar feature.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;mcluck:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;Very cool article. To really drill it home, I would have loved to see how the query plan changes. It _looks_ like it should Just Work(tm) but my brain refuses to believe that it&amp;#x27;s able to use those new indexes so flawlessly&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lex-2008:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;interesting, but can&amp;#x27;t you use &amp;quot;Index On Expression&amp;quot; &amp;lt;&lt;a href="https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;sqlite.org&amp;#x2F;expridx.html" rel="nofollow"&gt;https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;sqlite.org&amp;#x2F;expridx.html&lt;/a&gt;&amp;gt;?&lt;p&gt;i.e. something like this: CREATE INDEX idx_events_type ON events(json_extract(data, &amp;#x27;$.type&amp;#x27;))?&lt;p&gt;i guess caveat here is that slight change in json path syntax (can&amp;#x27;t think of any right now) can cause SQLite to not use this index, while in case of explicitly specified Virtual Generated Columns you&amp;#x27;re guaranteed to use the index.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div style="margin: 10px 0; padding: 10px; background: #f6f6f6; border-left: 3px solid #ff6600;"&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0 0 5px 0;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;upmostly:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin: 0;"&gt;I was inspired to write this blog post after reading bambax&amp;#x27;s comment on a HN post back in 2023: &lt;a href="https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&amp;#x2F;item?id=37082941"&gt;https:&amp;#x2F;&amp;#x2F;news.ycombinator.com&amp;#x2F;item?id=37082941&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.dbpro.app/blog/sqlite-json-virtual-columns-indexing</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 12 Dec 2025 13:25:19 +0000</pubDate>
      <comments>https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=46243904</comments>
    </item>
